From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 DisplayPort AUX/HPD PHY

Without an AUX channel there is no DPCD, no EDID and no link training, so of
everything the DP-alt path still needs (PD, a Type-C mux, a Trilinear DP
transmitter, TCON_TV, a KMS driver) this is the smallest piece with the
largest downstream effect -- and, unusually for this subsystem, the one whose
registers are fully documented.

The block is AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL0/1/2 at 0x06C01E00, three registers inside
the SERDES subsystem aperture. It is not part of the combo PHY: the UM block
diagram (Figure 18-3) draws AUX_HPD PHY beside
COMB0_PHY_SERDES, each with its own interface into the DP1.4/eDP controller,
and the UM gives it its own chapters 18.2.6.12-18.2.6.14.

An earlier register map listed this block as an open gap. It is not.
---------------------------------------------------------------

That map listed the block as

    | 0x1e00 | AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL0 | DP AUX/HPD analog front-end
      (def 0x00330000) |

i.e. one register, no fields, marked open. The A733 UM V0.92 in fact documents
all three registers field by field, with encodings and per-field defaults
(sections 18.2.6.12 = 0x1e00, .13 = 0x1e04, .14 = 0x1e08; the next section,
18.2.6.15, is already COMB1). So every value below is cited to a UM field row
rather than to the vendor.

Silicon, and what it is a witness of
------------------------------------

A register capture was taken on 2026-08-17 on
the 8 GiB board under the vendor 6.6 stack with a real DP-alt link up: a 1920x1200
monitor on Type-C J4, alt mode entered (SVID ff01, pin assignment C), HPD
asserted, plug orientation REVERSE. In it:

    0x06c01e00  0x03331131      (reset 0x00330000)
    0x06c01e04  0x00010e0d      (reset 0x0000000d)
    0x06c01e08  absent          (the dump lists non-zero words only, so = 0)

The same three registers were read at their reset values on this board twice
before: on our own 6.18.36 kernel and on the 2026-07-18 vendor capture, both
with nothing plugged into J4. So this block is
configured at alt-mode entry, not at boot, and that capture is the first
time anyone here has seen it in its working state. That is what makes it usable
as a target: it is not "a register that happens to read X", it is "the value a
board with a live AUX channel runs".

⚠ It is still a witness argument, not a mechanism proof. It says what a working
AUX front end looks like; it does not say which of these writes is the one that
matters, and a write whose value equals the reset default is invisible to this
method by construction -- five of the fourteen below are exactly that.

Why a new driver and not a second phy inside `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`
-------------------------------------------------

Both were considered against how `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` and `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy` are actually built, and the split
wins on four counts:

1. `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy` gives combophy0 `#phy-cells = <0>` with `of_phy_simple_xlate`, and dwc3
   references it as `phys = <&u2phy>, <&combophy0>`. A second phy on that
   provider needs `#phy-cells = <1>`, i.e. a cell-count change on the one node
   in this tree that is HW-proven at 10 Gbps in both plug orientations. Nothing
   about a DP front end justifies re-opening that; a bring-up patch that has
   never seen a board should not be the reason a working link is re-tested.

2. `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`'s header records the 2026-07-29 change that moved the SERDES subsystem
   gates and the companion PHY's reset-n *out* of that driver -- "they are a
   clock and a reset and they belong to the provider added in `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-subsystem-clocks` and to the
   companion PHY driver `phy-allwinner-a733-add-usb2-companion-phy`". Putting a second, unrelated register block back
   into the same driver would undo that lesson rather than extend it. AUX/HPD
   is a different register block, a different UM chapter and a different PHY
   instance in the UM's own block diagram.

3. The consumers and their lifetimes differ. combophy0's phy_ops.init has to run
   from dwc3, four lines before its core soft reset; this one runs from the DP
   transmitter on alt-mode entry, seconds later and possibly never. `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy`'s
   header already records what came of coupling this device's probe order to
   another's: a devlink ring that fw_devlink stopped honouring once the Type-C
   connector's graph closed.

4. The vendor models it the same way -- its own DT node, its own reg, its own
   phy_ops (aux_hpd_phy_ops) -- which is a hint, not evidence, and is listed
   last for that reason.

What argues for one driver is the shared SERDES clock and the shared SERDES
reset. That is already solved in this tree and needs no new mechanism: `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`
takes RST_BUS_SERDES with devm_reset_control_get_shared() and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-subsystem-clocks`'s provider
holds it deasserted, so a third shared consumer is the established pattern.
This driver takes it shared for the same reason and never sets the clock's rate
-- `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` owns the 100 MHz serdes-phy-cfg rate and both PHYs hang off that net.

Every write, and where it comes from
------------------------------------

Fourteen register writes: eleven that program fields, then three enable bits.
Order is the vendor's and is kept. "delta" = does the write change the register
when it starts from its documented reset value.

  #   reg      field                     val   delta  source
  --  -------  ------------------------  ----  -----  --------------------------
   1  CTRL0    [27:24] RX_HYST_LVL       0x3   yes    UM: 011 = 15 mV, marked
                                                      "(default)" in the encoding
                                                      table; register reset is 0
                                                      = no hysteresis
   2  CTRL0    [21:20] RX_DEGLITCH_FLT   0x3   no     UM: highest glitch-duration
                                                      rejection, = reset value
   3  CTRL0    [17:16] TX_SLEW           0x3   no     UM: 81 ps rise/fall,
                                                      = reset value
   4  CTRL0    [13:12] BG_START_ADJ      0x1   yes    UM: 01 = 558.5 mV bandgap
                                                      start-up sense, marked
                                                      "(default)"; reset is 0 =
                                                      543.0 mV
   5  CTRL0    [8] CFG_AUX_EN            1     yes    UM: enable AUX TX_EN/RX_EN
                                                      per CFG_AUX_TX_DIRECTION
   6  CTRL1    [28] RX_OFFSET_DISABLE    0     no     UM: loopback-only offset;
                                                      = reset value
   7  CTRL1    [24] TERM_HIGHZ_EN        0     no     UM: drive low in
                                                      mission mode, high only for
                                                      IDDQ testing
   8  CTRL1    [23:16] ATBSEL_ONEHOT     0x01  yes    ⚠ see below
   9  CTRL1    [12:8] TX_CUR_CTRL        0x0e  yes    UM: 0xE = 367/169 mVpp (DP
                                                      default); 0x13 = 428/230
                                                      mVpp (eDP default) would be wrong
                                                      here -- this board has no
                                                      eDP connector at all
  10  CTRL1    [4:0] TERM_SEG_EN         0x0d  no     UM: 94 ohm, marked
                                                      "(default)", field goal is
                                                      100 ohm differential;
                                                      = reset value
  11  CTRL2    whole register            0     no     UM: no AUX polarity
                                                      inversion either way,
                                                      HPD_GP_ANA 0, HPD_TX_OEN 0
                                                      (HPD pad stays an input),
                                                      TX_REDUCED_SWING 0 = DP
                                                      swing -- which has to agree
                                                      with #9, and does
  12  CTRL0    [4] DECAP_EN              1     yes    UM: with decap_en_del,
                                                      limits the inrush current
                                                      into the avdd decoupling
                                                      capacitor
  13  CTRL0    [5] DECAP_EN_DEL          1     yes    UM: delayed decap enable;
                                                      both decap bits have to be
                                                      high for the HPD circuitry
                                                      to work
  14  CTRL0    [0] BG_EN                 1     yes    UM: bandgap enable;
                                                      powers the AUX RX/TX bias

Accumulate them from the reset values and the arithmetic closes on the capture:

  CTRL0  0x00330000 -> #1 0x03330000 -> #4 0x03331000 -> #5 0x03331100
                    -> #12 0x03331110 -> #13 0x03331130 -> #14 0x03331131  ✓
  CTRL1  0x0000000d -> #8 0x0001000d -> #9 0x00010e0d                      ✓
  CTRL2  0x00000000 -> #11 0x00000000                                                ✓

Writes #2, #3, #6, #7, #10 are no-ops against a fresh block and are kept anyway:
nothing resets this block short of RST_BUS_SERDES, which USB3 holds deasserted
for the life of the system, so a second DP attach inherits whatever the first
one left.

The one write that is documented but not justified
--------------------------------------------------

#8. The honest form: the vendor writes 1 to bit 16 of 0x06C01E04; the UM does
document that field -- [23:16] `i_auxhpd_atbsel_onehot`, a one-hot ATB select,
where 0x1 picks the source voltage of the AUX receiver's differential pair -- but
that is an analog test bus tap, and no reason is given anywhere why a mission-mode driver
should enable one. Silicon with a live DP-alt link reads it set. It is
reproduced because the only known-good witness has it on, and dropping it would
both diverge from that witness and make the read-back check below meaningless.
If it is later shown inert, removing it is one line.

(The vendor sets bit 16 with a one-bit RMW; this driver writes the whole one-hot
field as 0x01. Same end state on silicon -- bits 23:17 read 0 there -- and
deterministic if some earlier owner left another code selected.)

Two more things sourced only to the vendor, stated as such:

 - the two `msleep(10)` calls, one at the top of .init and one at the top of
   .power_on. The UM documents no settling time for this block and names no rail
   this would wait on. Kept because a superfluous 10 ms costs 10 ms per DP
   attach and a missing one costs a board cycle.
 - the split itself -- clock and reset in .init, register programming in
   .power_on -- follows the vendor's aux_hpd_init/aux_hpd_power_on. It is also
   the right layering independently, and it is what the generic PHY API asks
   for, so it is not carried on the vendor's authority alone.

Board wiring, and what it settles
---------------------------------

DP-AUXP/AUXN go straight to Type-C J4 SBU1/SBU2 through 100 nF DC blocking caps
(C105/C107, sch p12 L38-44; SoC-side nets sch p6 L124-125). There is no external SBU mux and no redriver on
this board -- HUSB311 is a CC/PD controller only, not a lane crossbar.
DP-REXT = R68 3.01K (sch p6 L144-145).

So a reversed plug swaps AUX+ and AUX-, and something has to un-swap it. It is
not the two AUX polarity bits in CTRL2: the 8 GiB board's capture was taken with the plug
REVERSE -- COMB0 LINK_CTRL0 reads 0x00001131 in the same dump, bit12
TYPEC_CONN_DIR set -- and CTRL2 was still 0 while the link ran. Orientation is
therefore handled upstream of this block, and programming those bits per
orientation would break the one case that has been observed working.
Who does swap AUX on a reversed plug stays an open question; this
capture does not answer it, but it does rule this register out.

The DP-HPD pad (sch p6 L127) occurs on no other schematic page, while AUXP/AUXN
occur on both p6 and p12. Read as: HPD is not routed to the connector, and in DP
alt mode it arrives over CC as a DisplayPort Attention message. That changes
none of the writes -- the vendor enables the HPD circuitry regardless and
silicon agrees -- but nobody should expect a hotplug interrupt out of this PHY.

The AUX/HPD rail is `AVDD-HPD-AUX`, 3.3 V (sch p6 L130). We
have not traced it to a PMIC output, so no `*-supply` is wired here. A `regulator-fixed`
stand-in would satisfy the binding and prove nothing -- this tree has paid for
that once already -- so the rail is left unmodelled and named as open.

Register window overlap -- a real one, worked around on purpose
---------------------------------------------------------------

`arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy` gives combophy0 a first window of 0x06C01000 + 0x1000, which swallows this
block whole (0x06C01E00 + 0x200 ends at 0x06C01FFF), and
devm_platform_ioremap_resource() requests its region exclusively
(lib/devres.c:154), so this driver would probe with -EBUSY against a working
USB3 PHY.

This driver therefore maps with platform_get_resource() + devm_ioremap(), the
same non-exclusive pattern `phy-allwinner-a733-add-usb2-companion-phy` uses for the shared SYSCFG res_base. The clean
fix is on the other side -- combophy0's highest offset is LINK_STUS0 at 0x900,
its window only needs 0xa00, and the vendor DT uses exactly 0xa00 -- but
shrinking it edits the HW-proven USB3 node, so it belongs to a patch that
re-proves SuperSpeed rather than to this one. Recorded here so the next reader
finds the reason instead of the symptom.

The vendor's AUX behaviour, and why none of it is here
------------------------------------------------------

The vendor's DP stack talks on AUX at bind, blind, and retries for four and a
half seconds. Measured on the 8 GiB board booted from the vendor eMMC system with nothing
plugged into J4:

    eDP binds at 1.646 s and immediately issues DPCD reads
    34 x "edp wait aux reply timeout!" between 1.69 s and 6.21 s, in 5 rounds
    trilinear_aux_read retry 7 ... AUX_TIMEOUT
      -> edp_aux_read fail addr:0x0 len:0x10
      -> "fail to read edp dpcd from extcon sink"
      -> "typec aux communication failed after retry N"
    throughout: the vendor's own mux sits in STATE_SAFE and every extcon reads
    DP=0

The mechanism is in the vendor source, not guessed: edp_sink_extcon_evt() reacts
to an extcon event and on failure schedules drm_edp_typec_hpd_monitor() once a
second for up to 30 rounds (sunxi_drm_edp.c, ~359-412 and ~4429). Blind probe at
bind, plus a long blind retry loop.

This is behaviour to reject, and it is worth naming the likely reason it can
never have worked: with nothing attached, this block reads its reset value
0x00330000 -- observed under the vendor stack on 2026-07-18 and again on our own
kernel on 2026-08-17. Reset value means BG_EN = 0 and
CFG_AUX_EN = 0, i.e. the AUX transmitter and receiver bias were never enabled.
The vendor's aux_hpd power_on only runs on alt-mode entry, so those 34
transactions were issued into a front end that was still powered down. Two
separate observations, one inference; stated as an inference.

What this driver does about it: nothing, by construction, and the constraint is
recorded here so the future DP consumer inherits it.

 1. No AUX transaction at bind -- this driver issues none, ever. It programs
    analog trims and enables; the transactions belong to the DP transmitter.
 2. The .init/.power_on split is the hook that makes gating possible, and that
    is the reason to keep it. .init is clock and reset only and is safe at bind;
    .power_on is what turns on the bandgap, the AUX enable and the termination,
    and it is what the consumer must defer until DP alt mode has actually been
    entered and the mux is in a DP state. A consumer that calls phy_power_on()
    at bind gets the vendor's ordering back.
 3. No sink is a state, not an error. There is no error-level logging on any
    path here that "no sink" can reach: .init logs dev_err only if the clock or
    the reset genuinely fails, and .power_on's single line is dev_info and
    prints once per power-on, never in a loop.
 4. No retry loop, no poll, no timeout -- and none is possible: the whole block
    is three R/W configuration registers (0x1e00/04/08, then COMB1 at 0x2000).
    There is no status or ready bit in it to wait on, so the entire class of
    failure the vendor logs cannot originate in this driver. If a bounded retry
    is ever needed on the DP side, its bound and its reason belong in that
    patch's header.

What this patch does not do
---------------------------

No DT binding document (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` and `phy-allwinner-a733-add-usb2-companion-phy` have none either; owed for all three).
No consumer: the DP transmitter does not exist in this tree, so the phy provider
registers and nothing calls it. probe() deliberately touches no register at all
-- it maps, gets the clock and the reset, creates the phy and returns -- so an
unconsumed node costs exactly one platform device.

HW-pending, single observable
-----------------------------

With a DP sink on J4 and a consumer calling phy_init()/phy_power_on(), the
driver's own dev_info line must print

    CTRL0=0x03331131 CTRL1=0x00010e0d CTRL2=0x00000000

which is the vendor's live-link state read back off our own writes. Read
independently with `busybox devmem 0x06c01e00` / `0x06c01e04` / `0x06c01e08`;
matching all three is the proof, and a mismatch names the failing field
directly. That line is dev_info and not dev_dbg until it has been recorded;
demote it afterwards, do not delete it.

Negatively, and it has to be checked in the same session: USB3 must still
enumerate at SuperSpeed. This driver writes no register outside
0x06C01E00..0x06C01FFF and takes the SERDES reset shared, so it should not be
able to disturb it -- "should not" is the claim under test.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/Kconfig b/drivers/phy/allwinner/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/Kconfig
@@ -81,3 +81,16 @@
 	help
 	  Enable this for the USB2.0 UTMI companion PHY of the A733 (sun60iw2)
 	  USB3.1 DRD controller. Required for the dwc3 core to leave reset.
+
+config PHY_SUN60I_A733_AUX_HPD
+	tristate "Allwinner A733 DisplayPort AUX/HPD PHY"
+	depends on ARCH_SUNXI || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on HAS_IOMEM && OF
+	depends on RESET_CONTROLLER
+	select GENERIC_PHY
+	help
+	  Enable this for the DisplayPort AUX/HPD analog front end inside the
+	  A733 (sun60iw2) SERDES subsystem. It drives the AUX differential pair
+	  and the HPD pin, so without it a sink cannot be reached over AUX and
+	  there is no DPCD, no EDID and no link training. Separate from the
+	  COMB0 combo-PHY, which carries the DP main-link lanes.
diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/Makefile b/drivers/phy/allwinner/Makefile
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/Makefile
@@ -5,3 +5,4 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_SUN50I_USB3)		+= phy-sun50i-usb3.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_COMBO)	+= phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_USB2)	+= phy-sun60i-a733-usb2.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_PHY_SUN60I_A733_AUX_HPD)	+= phy-sun60i-a733-aux-hpd.o
diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-aux-hpd.c b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-aux-hpd.c
new file mode 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-aux-hpd.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-aux-hpd.c
@@ -0,0 +1,460 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) DisplayPort AUX/HPD PHY.
+ *
+ * The A733 SERDES subsystem contains four PHY instances, and the UM block
+ * diagram (Figure 18-3) draws them as peers: the AUX_HPD
+ * PHY sits beside COMB0_PHY_SERDES with its own interface into the DP1.4/eDP
+ * controller. It is not part of the combo PHY -- it is the low-speed analog
+ * front end for the DP AUX differential pair and the HPD pin, and it has its own
+ * three-register block at 0x06C01E00 (SERDES_SUBSYS_APP + 0x1e00..0x1e08,
+ * UM 18.2.6.12-18.2.6.14).
+ *
+ * Without it there is no AUX channel: no DPCD read, no EDID, no link training.
+ * It is therefore the smallest piece of the DP-alt path, and the first one that
+ * can be written against a fully documented register set -- every field
+ * programmed here comes from the UM field tables, not from a decoded silicon
+ * dump (the Cadence PMA body next door is the opposite case).
+ *
+ * Register-block ownership, the lesson `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` already paid for: this driver
+ * touches 0x06C01E00..0x06C01FFF and nothing else. It does not write the COMB0
+ * link registers, it does not write the SERDES subsystem gates, and it does not
+ * touch a single register on the USB3 path.
+ *
+ * The SERDES config clock and the SERDES block reset are shared with the COMB0
+ * combo PHY (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`) and the serdes-ccu (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-subsystem-clocks`). The reset is taken *shared* for
+ * the same reason `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` takes it shared: those two hold it deasserted, and
+ * asserting it from here would take their registers away mid-flight. The clock
+ * is enabled but its rate is never set -- `phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy` owns the 100 MHz serdes-phy-cfg
+ * rate, and both personalities run off that one net.
+ *
+ * phy_ops split follows the block: .init is plumbing (clock + reset), .power_on
+ * is the analog bring-up. The phy core serialises both under phy->mutex, so this
+ * driver carries no lock of its own.
+ *
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * A constraint for whoever writes the DP consumer, because the vendor gets it
+ * wrong and the failure is expensive to diagnose from the other end.
+ *
+ * The vendor's DP stack issues DPCD reads at bind and retries blind: measured on
+ * the 8 GiB board with nothing plugged into Type-C J4, eDP binds at 1.646 s and logs
+ * 34 x "edp wait aux reply timeout!" between 1.69 s and 6.21 s, in five rounds,
+ * while its own mux sits in STATE_SAFE and every extcon reads DP=0. The retry is
+ * edp_sink_extcon_evt() rescheduling drm_edp_typec_hpd_monitor() once a second
+ * for up to 30 rounds. Four and a half seconds of every boot, talking to a sink
+ * that is not there.
+ *
+ * So:
+ *  - Nothing here talks on AUX. This driver only programs the analog front end;
+ *    the transactions are the DP transmitter's.
+ *  - phy_power_on() is the gate, and that is why the ops are split. .init
+ *    (clock + reset) is safe at bind. .power_on turns on the bandgap, the AUX
+ *    enable and the termination, and must be deferred until DP alt mode has
+ *    actually been entered and the mux is in a DP state. Calling it at bind
+ *    reproduces the vendor's ordering.
+ *  - "No sink" is a state, not an error: no path reachable without a sink logs
+ *    at error level, and there is no retry loop here at all. Nor could there be
+ *    one -- the block is three R/W config registers with no status or ready bit
+ *    to poll, so no AUX timeout can originate in this driver.
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ */
+
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
+#include <linux/bits.h>
+#include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/phy/phy.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
+
+/*
+ * AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL0, offset 0x00, reset 0x0033_0000 (UM 18.2.6.12).
+ * The UM's reserved rows print as 23:21 / 19:17 / 11:10 / 7:6 / 3:1, i.e. they
+ * overlap the field row below them by one bit. That is an extraction artefact of
+ * the PDF, not two readings of the silicon: the reset value 0x00330000 has
+ * exactly bits 21,20,17,16 set, which is only self-consistent if TX_SLEW is
+ * [17:16] and RX_DEGLITCH_FILTER is [21:20] as their own rows say. The field
+ * rows are used; the reserved rows are not.
+ */
+#define AUX_HPD_CTRL0			0x00
+#define  CTRL0_RX_HYST_LVL		GENMASK(27, 24)
+#define  CTRL0_RX_DEGLITCH_FILTER	GENMASK(21, 20)
+#define  CTRL0_TX_SLEW			GENMASK(17, 16)
+#define  CTRL0_BG_START_ADJ		GENMASK(13, 12)
+#define  CTRL0_CFG_AUX_TX_DIRECTION	BIT(9)
+#define  CTRL0_CFG_AUX_EN		BIT(8)
+#define  CTRL0_DECAP_EN_DEL		BIT(5)
+#define  CTRL0_DECAP_EN			BIT(4)
+#define  CTRL0_BG_EN			BIT(0)
+
+/* AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL1, offset 0x04, reset 0x0000_000D (UM 18.2.6.13). */
+#define AUX_HPD_CTRL1			0x04
+#define  CTRL1_RX_OFFSET_DISABLE	BIT(28)
+#define  CTRL1_TERM_HIGHZ_EN		BIT(24)
+#define  CTRL1_ATBSEL_ONEHOT		GENMASK(23, 16)
+#define  CTRL1_TX_CUR_CTRL		GENMASK(12, 8)
+#define  CTRL1_TERM_SEG_EN		GENMASK(4, 0)
+
+/*
+ * AUX_HPD_PHY_PINS_CTRL2, offset 0x08, reset 0x0000_0000 (UM 18.2.6.14):
+ * bit29 AUX_DATA_OUT_POLAR_SEL, bit28 AUX_DATA_IN_POLAR_SEL, [11:8] HPD_GP_ANA,
+ * bit4 HPD_TX_OEN, bit0 TX_REDUCED_SWING. All zero = AUX polarity not inverted,
+ * HPD pad output disabled (HPD is an input, driven by the sink), and normal DP
+ * transmitter swing rather than the reduced eDP swing.
+ */
+#define AUX_HPD_CTRL2			0x08
+
+/* Field values, all from the UM encodings cited per write in _power_on(). */
+#define RX_HYST_LVL_15MV		0x3	/* code 011 */
+#define RX_DEGLITCH_HIGHEST		0x3
+#define TX_SLEW_81PS			0x3
+#define BG_START_ADJ_558MV		0x1
+#define ATBSEL_RX_DIFF_PAIR_SRC		0x01
+#define TX_CUR_CTRL_DP			0x0e	/* 367/169 mVpp, "DP default" */
+#define TERM_SEG_EN_94R			0x0d	/* 94 ohm, targets 100 ohm diff */
+
+/*
+ * Vendor sleeps 10 ms at the top of both its aux_hpd init and its power_on. The
+ * UM documents no settling time for this block and names no rail this would be
+ * waiting on, so the number has no source beyond the vendor. It is kept because
+ * a superfluous 10 ms costs ten milliseconds per DP attach and a missing one
+ * costs a board cycle; when the DP path has its own silicon witness, removing it
+ * is a measurable experiment rather than a guess.
+ */
+#define AUX_HPD_SETTLE_MS		10
+
+struct a733_aux_hpd {
+	struct device		*dev;
+	void __iomem		*base;
+	struct clk		*clk;
+	struct reset_control	*reset;
+};
+
+static void aux_hpd_rmw(struct a733_aux_hpd *p, u32 off, u32 mask, u32 val)
+{
+	u32 v = readl(p->base + off);
+
+	v &= ~mask;
+	v |= val & mask;
+	writel(v, p->base + off);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The analog bring-up. Every write below is one UM field row; the comment says
+ * where the value comes from and whether it changes the register.
+ *
+ * The order is load-bearing and is the vendor's: eleven field writes first, then
+ * the three enable bits, DECAP_EN before DECAP_EN_DEL before BG_EN. The UM says
+ * why -- decap_en and decap_en_del together limit the inrush into the avdd
+ * decoupling capacitor while it charges, and the UM wants both high for the
+ * HPD circuitry. Asserting them before the trims are in place,
+ * or in one write, defeats the staging.
+ *
+ * End state on silicon, from a vendor 6.6 stack with a live DP-alt link on
+ * the 8 GiB board (1920x1200 monitor on Type-C J4, HPD asserted, read back
+ * on 2026-08-17):
+ *
+ *	0x06c01e00 = 0x03331131   (reset 0x00330000)
+ *	0x06c01e04 = 0x00010e0d   (reset 0x0000000d)
+ *	0x06c01e08 = 0x00000000   (reset 0x00000000, absent from the non-zero dump)
+ *
+ * The writes below accumulate to exactly those three values from those three
+ * reset values. That is the read-back this driver owes.
+ */
+static int a733_aux_hpd_power_on(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	struct a733_aux_hpd *p = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
+
+	msleep(AUX_HPD_SETTLE_MS);
+
+	/*
+	 * CTRL0 trims. RX_HYST_LVL / RX_DEGLITCH_FILTER / TX_SLEW /
+	 * BG_START_ADJ are each set to the value the UM's own encoding table
+	 * marks "(default)", which for two of them is not the register's reset
+	 * value:
+	 *
+	 *  [27:24] RX_HYST_LVL   = 3 -> 15 mV AUX receiver hysteresis. Reset is
+	 *                          0 = no hysteresis; 011 is the UM's marked
+	 *                          default. Changes the register.
+	 *  [21:20] RX_DEGLITCH   = 3 -> highest glitch-duration rejection. UM
+	 *                          default AND reset value: a no-op write, kept
+	 *                          so the state is programmed, not inherited.
+	 *  [17:16] TX_SLEW       = 3 -> 81 ps AUX TX rise/fall. UM default AND
+	 *                          reset value; same reason.
+	 *  [13:12] BG_START_ADJ  = 1 -> 558.5 mV bandgap start-up sense voltage.
+	 *                          Reset is 0 = 543.0 mV; 01 is the UM's marked
+	 *                          default. Changes the register.
+	 *
+	 * The two no-ops matter because nothing resets this block short of
+	 * RST_BUS_SERDES, which USB3 holds deasserted for the life of the
+	 * system: a second DP attach finds whatever the first one left behind.
+	 */
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_RX_HYST_LVL,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL0_RX_HYST_LVL, RX_HYST_LVL_15MV));
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_RX_DEGLITCH_FILTER,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL0_RX_DEGLITCH_FILTER, RX_DEGLITCH_HIGHEST));
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_TX_SLEW,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL0_TX_SLEW, TX_SLEW_81PS));
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_BG_START_ADJ,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL0_BG_START_ADJ, BG_START_ADJ_558MV));
+
+	/*
+	 * bit8 CFG_AUX_EN = 1: turns on AUX TX_EN and RX_EN, gated by
+	 * CFG_AUX_TX_DIRECTION; bit9 CFG_AUX_TX_DIRECTION is deliberately left
+	 * at its reset 0 = RX enabled, TX disabled -- AUX is half-duplex and the
+	 * direction is flipped per transaction by whoever drives the channel.
+	 * The vendor PHY driver never writes bit9 either; whichever block turns
+	 * the pair around, it is not this one.
+	 */
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_CFG_AUX_EN, CTRL0_CFG_AUX_EN);
+
+	/*
+	 * CTRL1.
+	 *
+	 *  bit28 RX_OFFSET_DISABLE = 0 -> the loopback-only common-mode offset
+	 *        stays at its reset value. Written explicitly because loopback
+	 *        is a test mode and mission mode wants it off.
+	 *  bit24 TERM_HIGHZ_EN     = 0 -> on-chip termination driven, not
+	 *        high-z. The UM is unusually direct here: low for mission
+	 *        mode, high only for IDDQ testing.
+	 */
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL1, CTRL1_RX_OFFSET_DISABLE, 0);
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL1, CTRL1_TERM_HIGHZ_EN, 0);
+
+	/*
+	 * [23:16] ATBSEL_ONEHOT = 0x01. This is the one write here that is
+	 * documented but not justified. The UM calls the field
+	 * i_auxhpd_atbsel_onehot, a one-hot ATB select, with
+	 * 0x1 picking the source voltage of the AUX receiver pair -- an analog test bus
+	 * tap, which a mission-mode driver has no reason to turn on. It is
+	 * reproduced anyway because the only silicon we have with a working AUX
+	 * channel has it set (0x06c01e04 bit16 = 1 with a live link), and
+	 * deviating from the sole known-good witness on a hunch is how a PHY
+	 * stops training. Reset value is 0, so this does change the register.
+	 *
+	 * The vendor sets bit16 with a one-bit read-modify-write; the whole
+	 * one-hot field is written here instead. Same end state on silicon --
+	 * bits 23:17 read 0 there -- and deterministic if some earlier owner
+	 * left a different code selected.
+	 */
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL1, CTRL1_ATBSEL_ONEHOT,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL1_ATBSEL_ONEHOT, ATBSEL_RX_DIFF_PAIR_SRC));
+
+	/*
+	 * [12:8] TX_CUR_CTRL = 0x0E. The UM's encoding table annotates exactly
+	 * two of the 32 codes: 0xE = 367/169 mVpp (the DP default) and 0x13 =
+	 * 428/230 mVpp (the eDP default). This board's only display path is DP over
+	 * Type-C J4 -- the schematic has no eDP connector on any page
+	 * -- so
+	 * 0xE is the sourced value and 0x13 would be the wrong one. It has to
+	 * agree with CTRL2 bit0 TX_REDUCED_SWING = 0
+	 * (0 = normal DP swing) below, and it does.
+	 *
+	 * [4:0] TERM_SEG_EN = 0x0D -> 94 ohm, the UM's marked default, out of a
+	 * field that aims at a 100 ohm differential
+	 * termination. Equals the reset value: a no-op on a fresh block, a
+	 * repair on a re-attach.
+	 */
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL1, CTRL1_TX_CUR_CTRL,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL1_TX_CUR_CTRL, TX_CUR_CTRL_DP));
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL1, CTRL1_TERM_SEG_EN,
+		    FIELD_PREP(CTRL1_TERM_SEG_EN, TERM_SEG_EN_94R));
+
+	/*
+	 * CTRL2 = 0 as a whole register, which is what the vendor writes and
+	 * what silicon reads on a live link. Field by field: no AUX data
+	 * polarity inversion in either direction, HPD_GP_ANA 0, HPD_TX_OEN 0
+	 * (the HPD pad stays an input) and TX_REDUCED_SWING 0 = DP swing.
+	 *
+	 * Worth stating because it looks like an omission: the AUX pair is wired
+	 * straight to Type-C SBU1/SBU2 through 100 nF DC blocking caps with no
+	 * external mux (schematic p12 L38-44, C105/C107 100NF 10V; the SoC-side
+	 * nets DP-AUXP/AUXN are p6 L124-125), so a reversed plug swaps AUX+ and
+	 * AUX- and something has to un-swap it. It is not these two polarity
+	 * bits: the 8 GiB board's capture was taken with the plug REVERSE -- COMB0
+	 * LINK_CTRL0 reads 0x00001131, bit12 TYPEC_CONN_DIR set -- and CTRL2 was
+	 * still 0 while the link ran. So orientation is handled upstream of this
+	 * block, and writing these bits per orientation would break the one case
+	 * that has actually been observed working.
+	 */
+	writel(0, p->base + AUX_HPD_CTRL2);
+
+	/*
+	 * The three enables, last and in this order. bit4 DECAP_EN then bit5
+	 * DECAP_EN_DEL is the inrush staging the UM describes (a delayed decap
+	 * enable that limits the inrush current into the avdd decoupling
+	 * capacitor); reversing them, or merging them into one write,
+	 * charges the decoupling cap in a single step. bit0 BG_EN goes last and
+	 * powers up the AUX RX/TX bias -- it
+	 * is the switch that makes everything programmed above take effect.
+	 */
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_DECAP_EN, CTRL0_DECAP_EN);
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_DECAP_EN_DEL, CTRL0_DECAP_EN_DEL);
+	aux_hpd_rmw(p, AUX_HPD_CTRL0, CTRL0_BG_EN, CTRL0_BG_EN);
+
+	/*
+	 * This patch has exactly one observable and this line is it, so it is
+	 * dev_info and not dev_dbg until the comparison has been made on a
+	 * board: CTRL0 must read 0x03331131, CTRL1 0x00010e0d, CTRL2 0x00000000.
+	 * Demote it once that is recorded -- do not delete it.
+	 */
+	dev_info(p->dev,
+		 "AUX/HPD on: CTRL0=0x%08x CTRL1=0x%08x CTRL2=0x%08x (want 0x03331131 0x00010e0d 0x00000000)\n",
+		 readl(p->base + AUX_HPD_CTRL0),
+		 readl(p->base + AUX_HPD_CTRL1),
+		 readl(p->base + AUX_HPD_CTRL2));
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Deliberately a no-op, and deliberately not "symmetric".
+ *
+ * The obvious power_off -- drop BG_EN, DECAP_EN_DEL, DECAP_EN and CFG_AUX_EN --
+ * would save the AUX bias current between DP sessions and is what a reviewer
+ * expects to see. It is not written because it has no witness of any kind: the
+ * vendor's power_off is empty, so the only unplug/replug behaviour ever observed
+ * on this block is "the analog front end stays enabled and power_on runs again".
+ * power_on above is idempotent -- every write is an absolute field write, no
+ * toggles -- so re-running it over a still-enabled block is exactly the case the
+ * vendor stack exercises.
+ *
+ * Turning the bias off here is four lines and one board test away; guessing at
+ * it now would put an unproven power-down in front of an unproven bring-up.
+ */
+static int a733_aux_hpd_power_off(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int a733_aux_hpd_init(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	struct a733_aux_hpd *p = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
+	int ret;
+
+	msleep(AUX_HPD_SETTLE_MS);
+
+	/*
+	 * Enable only -- the rate belongs to the COMB0 combo PHY, which sets
+	 * serdes-phy-cfg to 100 MHz (`phy-allwinner-a733-add-serdes-combophy`). Both PHYs hang off that one clock,
+	 * so a clk_set_rate() from here would be two drivers fighting over one
+	 * net.
+	 */
+	ret = clk_prepare_enable(p->clk);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(p->dev, "serdes clk enable failed: %d\n", ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Shared, and that is the point: combophy0 and the serdes-ccu each hold
+	 * a deassert on this line, so this one is a refcount bump and the
+	 * matching assert in .exit can never pull the SERDES block -- and with
+	 * it the running USB3 controller -- out from under them.
+	 */
+	ret = reset_control_deassert(p->reset);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(p->dev, "serdes reset deassert failed: %d\n", ret);
+		clk_disable_unprepare(p->clk);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int a733_aux_hpd_exit(struct phy *phy)
+{
+	struct a733_aux_hpd *p = phy_get_drvdata(phy);
+
+	reset_control_assert(p->reset);
+	clk_disable_unprepare(p->clk);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct phy_ops a733_aux_hpd_ops = {
+	.init		= a733_aux_hpd_init,
+	.exit		= a733_aux_hpd_exit,
+	.power_on	= a733_aux_hpd_power_on,
+	.power_off	= a733_aux_hpd_power_off,
+	.owner		= THIS_MODULE,
+};
+
+static int a733_aux_hpd_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct a733_aux_hpd *p;
+	struct phy_provider *provider;
+	struct resource *res;
+	struct phy *phy;
+
+	p = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*p), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!p)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	p->dev = dev;
+
+	/*
+	 * devm_ioremap(), not devm_platform_ioremap_resource(), and the reason
+	 * is a real overlap rather than tidiness: `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy` gives combophy0 a first
+	 * window of 0x06C01000 + 0x1000, which swallows this block whole
+	 * (0x06C01E00 + 0x200 ends at 0x06C01FFF). devm_ioremap_resource()
+	 * requests its region exclusively and would fail here with -EBUSY
+	 * against a driver that is HW-proven at 10 Gbps.
+	 *
+	 * The clean fix is on the other side -- combophy0's window only needs
+	 * 0xa00, its highest offset being LINK_STUS0 at 0x900, and the vendor DT
+	 * uses exactly 0xa00 -- but shrinking it edits the proven USB3 node, so
+	 * it belongs in a patch that re-proves SuperSpeed, not in this one. Same
+	 * non-exclusive mapping pattern `phy-allwinner-a733-add-usb2-companion-phy` uses for the shared SYSCFG
+	 * res_base.
+	 */
+	res = platform_get_resource(pdev, IORESOURCE_MEM, 0);
+	if (!res)
+		return -ENODEV;
+	p->base = devm_ioremap(dev, res->start, resource_size(res));
+	if (!p->base)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	p->clk = devm_clk_get(dev, "serdes");
+	if (IS_ERR(p->clk))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(p->clk), "serdes clk\n");
+
+	p->reset = devm_reset_control_get_shared(dev, NULL);
+	if (IS_ERR(p->reset))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(p->reset), "serdes reset\n");
+
+	phy = devm_phy_create(dev, NULL, &a733_aux_hpd_ops);
+	if (IS_ERR(phy))
+		return PTR_ERR(phy);
+	phy_set_drvdata(phy, p);
+
+	/*
+	 * Nothing above this line touched a register, and that is intentional:
+	 * with no DP consumer in the tree yet this driver probes, registers a
+	 * provider and stops. The first write happens when a consumer calls
+	 * phy_init() / phy_power_on().
+	 */
+	provider = devm_of_phy_provider_register(dev, of_phy_simple_xlate);
+	return PTR_ERR_OR_ZERO(provider);
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id a733_aux_hpd_of_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-aux-hpd-phy" },
+	{ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, a733_aux_hpd_of_match);
+
+static struct platform_driver a733_aux_hpd_driver = {
+	.probe	= a733_aux_hpd_probe,
+	.driver	= {
+		.name		= "sun60i-a733-aux-hpd-phy",
+		.of_match_table	= a733_aux_hpd_of_match,
+	},
+};
+module_platform_driver(a733_aux_hpd_driver);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Allwinner A733 DisplayPort AUX/HPD PHY");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
